    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Combination of Functions Given the Necessary Data",
    "problem": "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-6$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-6$ | $-2\\pi $ | $3\\pi $ | $5$ | $-2$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+2g(x))$ at $x=-6.$",
    "worked_solution": "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+2g(x))={f}^{′}(x)+2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-6$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+2g(x))|}_{x=-6} & ={f}^{′}(-6)+2{g}^{′}(-6) \\\\  & =5+2(-2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
